  "live_records": {
    "q-10841": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10841",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}-6x$ at the point $(3,-9).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-6x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-6x)=2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(3)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(3)=2(3)-6=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=0$ and the coordinates of our point $(3,-9),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-9) & =0(x-3) \\\\ y & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
