    "q-342351": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342351",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}$ at the point  $(2,1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2}^{3}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-2) \\\\ y & =2x-4+1 \\\\ y & =2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
