    "q-10838": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10838",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}+5x$ at the point $(-1,-4).$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5x)=2x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1)=2(-1)+5=3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=3$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y & =3x+3-4 \\\\ y & =3x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-10838-a",
