    "q-342365": {
      "dom_id": "step-q342365",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,-4).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=-2\\sqrt{x}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(4)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the coordinates of our point $(4,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
