    "q-10831": {
      "dom_id": "step-q10831",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find where the tangent to the curve  $y=\\frac{5}{x}-2x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{5}{x}-2x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{x}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{-1}-2x) \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-5(1)^{-2}-2=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-7(x-1) \\\\ y & =-7x+7+3 \\\\ y & =-7x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7(0)+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,10).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
